    "result_text": "Stewards can now reopen a closed period, and the Playwright test passes on `bin/e2e`.\n\n**What happens on reopen:**\n- **Steward:** the period shows as open again and its due-total is live. Here the rate rose from 10% to 20% while P1 was closed, so its total went from 1.00 to 2.00. The audit file gets `PERIOD_REOPENED sheet=<id> by=<user> period=<name>`.\n- **Anyone else:** refused with an error on the period's row, and nothing is stored or audited.\n- **A period that isn't closed:** also refused with the row error.\n\n**Changes:**\n- **Stored data:** reopening sets `closed` back to false and clears the saved closing total. The existing columns can hold that, so no database migration was needed.\n- **Server:** a new `POST /api/sheets/{sheetId}/periods/{name}/reopen` endpoint. It returns 403 for a non-steward and 409 for a period that isn't closed. The logic is in `PeriodService.reopen`.\n- **Front end:** a `period-reopen` button on every period row, open or closed, and a `reopen()` call in the periods store.\n\n**Decision for you:** the Reopen button shows on open periods too. The test clicks Reopen on a period that has just been reopened and expects the error, so the button has to be there. Hiding it on open periods would need the test changed.\n\nClaims dated in a reopened period can be edited again, because claim changes are only blocked while the period is closed.",
